Subject: [PATCH] T23 life-guard fault-tolerance: bounded IPC, encoder stall recovery, verified+honoured day/night switch, persisted forced mode
Target: prudynt-t @ f4b32289359d176fb0795d9f3735af0bd9d6d2fd (branch stable).
Stacks on: 0001-0004. Applies with git apply -p1 / patch -p1 AFTER them (verified).

Addresses the QA review's High/Medium fault-tolerance gaps for 24/7 unattended use:

H1 (prudyntctl.cpp) — bound the IPC socket: connect_sock() sets SO_RCVTIMEO/
   SO_SNDTIMEO (5s). The single DayNightWorker thread shells out to
   /sbin/daynight -> color -> prudyntctl; an unbounded read on a wedged IPC
   server would otherwise freeze ALL future day/night switches. Now bounded.

H2 (VideoWorker.cpp) — bounded stall recovery: stock prudynt incremented
   error_count on every GetStream failure / PollingStream timeout but NEVER
   acted on it, so a wedged encoder = a silent permanent black live view. Now,
   if no encoded frame is produced for kStallRestartMs (8s), request the proven
   pipeline rebuild (global_restart_video + cv notify, under mutex_main). Reset
   on every delivered frame and on subscriber (re)connect so an idle, viewer-
   less stream never trips it.

M5 (DayNightWorker.cpp + /sbin/daynight) — honour the optics-switch result:
   apply_mode() now returns bool (the /sbin/daynight exit status), and all four
   live callers advance current/live_mode ONLY on success — a failed actuation
   (stuck IR-cut, dead IR LED, hung/timed-out script) no longer reports a mode
   the camera did not physically reach; it holds the prior mode and retries on
   the next tick. The forced-mode request is consumed only on success.

M4 (JsonAPI.cpp) — persist a pinned mode: the force_mode API handler now mirrors
   the runtime atomic into the persisted force_mode_cfg member (bound to the
   "daynight.force_mode" config key), so an operator's pinned day/night survives
   a reboot instead of silently reverting to auto.

All changes are platform-agnostic C++ (no T23-only guards needed); they only add
fault-handling around existing behaviour.

